WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.088
Picture a notice. It tells a person receiving home and

00:00:03.088 --> 00:00:06.175
community-based services that a reassessment is coming. It gives a

00:00:06.175 --> 00:00:09.263
date. Somewhere in the third paragraph, it says the person

00:00:09.263 --> 00:00:12.351
can ask for a different date. Many people never find

00:00:12.351 --> 00:00:15.439
that sentence. Some miss the visit. Some lose time with

00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:17.600
their services while it gets sorted out.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.292
It is easy to think the fix is better writing.

00:00:21.292 --> 00:00:24.634
Shorter sentences. Friendlier words. A good readability score. Those help.

00:00:24.634 --> 00:00:27.977
But a readability score measures sentence length. It does not

00:00:27.977 --> 00:00:31.319
tell you whether a reader can find an option and

00:00:31.319 --> 00:00:32.990
act on it in time.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.728
Consider what happened when the IRS tested its own notices.

00:00:36.728 --> 00:00:40.116
Researchers mailed different versions to more than thirty-five thousand people

00:00:40.116 --> 00:00:43.505
who seemed eligible for a tax credit but had not

00:00:43.505 --> 00:00:46.893
claimed it. A simpler notice raised the share who claimed

00:00:46.893 --> 00:00:50.281
from fourteen percent to twenty-three percent. Adding the possible benefit

00:00:50.281 --> 00:00:53.669
amount raised it to thirty-one percent. Messages meant to make

00:00:53.669 --> 00:00:56.380
people feel less stigma did nothing at all.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.490
Clarity about the action mattered. Reassurance did not.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.683
Now add language. Plain English is not language access. DHS

00:01:04.683 --> 00:01:08.526
policy says county, Tribal and state servicing agencies must provide

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:12.369
appropriate language assistance. No one should be denied access to

00:01:12.369 --> 00:01:16.213
a DHS program because they do not speak English fluently.

00:01:16.213 --> 00:01:20.056
The National CLAS Standards call for free language assistance as

00:01:20.056 --> 00:01:20.440
well.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:24.319
Who interprets matters too. In one study of audiotaped emergency

00:01:24.319 --> 00:01:27.849
visits, untrained interpreters, such as family members or staff who

00:01:27.849 --> 00:01:31.378
happened to speak the language, made consequential errors as often

00:01:31.378 --> 00:01:34.908
as having no interpreter at all. Trained professional interpreters made

00:01:34.908 --> 00:01:38.437
far fewer. Those with at least one hundred hours of

00:01:38.437 --> 00:01:41.966
training made the fewest. A helpful bilingual coworker is generous.

00:01:41.966 --> 00:01:44.790
That coworker is not a language access plan.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.209
So how do you design a notice that adapts? Start

00:01:48.209 --> 00:01:51.278
with the decision the reader faces. Ask a few readers

00:01:51.278 --> 00:01:54.347
to show you what they would do next. Include people

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:57.416
who missed the step before. Include people who read the

00:01:57.416 --> 00:02:00.485
translated versions, and people who use accessible formats like screen

00:02:00.485 --> 00:02:02.020
readers. Watch where they stop.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.479
Some content will be required. Do not cut it to

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:08.588
make a test look better. Ask the person or area

00:02:08.588 --> 00:02:11.697
responsible what must stay. Then redesign around it. A clear

00:02:11.697 --> 00:02:14.806
heading. A short summary. A box that says, here is

00:02:14.806 --> 00:02:16.050
what to do next.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.770
Be honest about one limit. Co-design, involving people in designing

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:23.141
the services they use, has rarely been tested for outcomes.

00:02:23.141 --> 00:02:26.511
One review found it can make materials more relevant. It

00:02:26.511 --> 00:02:29.882
also found that some participants felt their input was ignored.

00:02:29.882 --> 00:02:33.252
So the last step matters most. Tell the people who

00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:36.623
helped what you changed, what you could not change, and

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:36.960
why.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:40.453
That is how a service learns to adapt, one tested

00:02:40.453 --> 00:02:41.710
notice at a time.
